0041 — a token that does not parse, in a literal that is the whole value of
a `class=` attribute, is a build error naming every such token, where it
dropped the literal and the node's every other utility with it.

`parseClassLiteral` returns null at the first token that does not parse, and
null drops the literal: the node gets none of the utilities in it. That is
upstream's prose rule — every string literal in a module is offered as a
candidate, so a sentence must compile to nothing without a word — and it
stays the rule wherever there is no other evidence. 0012 records the other
evidence: the literals that are, on some execution, the whole value of a
`class=` attribute (`fromClassAttribute`). Such a literal IS a class. With
it, the parser now gathers every token that does not parse and refuses the
literal naming all of them, together with the `animate-*` faults 0011
reports for the same literal; `compileClasses` gathers the refusals of one
compile into one `UncompiledClassError`, so one build names every refused
literal; and `tools/build.ts` puts each at its file, line and column in the
format `--strict-classes` already printed:

  error: main.tsx:5:15: class "absolute left-[1e-14px] w-[100px] rotate--18" does not compile: unknown utilities left-[1e-14px], rotate--18
    `left-[1e-14px]`: a number in exponent notation is not read — `left-[0.00000000000001px]` compiles.
    `rotate--18`: `rotate-N` takes N >= 0 degrees with no brackets or unit, and 342 is the same angle as -18 — `rotate-342` compiles.

Measured on the vendored compiler before the change, every literal beside
`absolute w-[100px]` and with `fromClassAttribute`: `left-[1e-14px]`,
`left-[1.2e3px]`, `opacity-[1e-7]`, `translate-x-[1e-3px]`,
`rotate-[-18deg]`, `rotate--18`, `-rotate-18`, `rotate-[342deg]`,
`-left-[12px]`, `-translate-x-4`, `text-[#12345]` and `fooo` each dropped
the literal and threw nothing; `left-[12.5px]`, `left-[-12px]`,
`translate-x-[-16px]`, `rotate-342` and `rotate-400` compiled. On the motion
repository's host, which is strict, such a node was not silent but was
misdiagnosed: mount threw `unknown class "…" - not in the compiled style
table (dynamic classes must be ternaries of full literals)`, which names
neither the token nor the cause, and only for a literal mount reaches — a
ternary arm a mount-only film never takes was reported by nothing. A native
host keeps no style for the node and counts a miss.

What a message says about a token it says from the token alone, and a form
it offers is one it has parsed: three rewrites are tried in order — a
number in exponent notation written out in decimal, Tailwind's leading `-`
moved inside the brackets, a negative or bracketed `rotate-` as `rotate-N`
with N in [0, 360), which is the only rotate form this compiler reads
(`rotate-[342deg]` does not parse) — and the result is named as compiling
only when `unknownUtilities` finds nothing in it; otherwise the message says
it does not compile either (`opacity-[1e-7]`: `opacity-N` takes no
brackets). A hex colour that is not 3, 6 or 8 digits and a variant prefix
other than `focus:`/`active:` are named without a rewrite. A refusal over a
number in exponent notation adds that a JS number prints that way through
`String(n)` or a template literal below 1e-6 and from 1e21 in magnitude.

Upstream behaviour that changes. A whole-value class literal that did not
compile was a warning and a bundle unless `--strict-classes`; it now fails
every build. `tests/class-diagnostics.test.ts`'s default-mode case pinned
the warning and the bundle, and is rewritten to pin the same three located
lines as errors and no bundle; the constant beside them is still a warning.
The left operand of `&&` is never the value, so it keeps upstream's warning
and `--strict-classes` error, and a new case pins both. The strict case is
unchanged and green. `site/content/docs/styling.md` and `--strict-classes`'s
help say which literals fail either way. Prose without provenance compiles
to nothing as before (`animate-imagination`, "Tap to start").

Swept before it landed: every `.tsx` under the motion repository's
`compositions/`, `src/`, `skills/` and `tests/` (69 files, 8,140 class=
literals) and under this tree at 66caf4e5 (120 files, 1,066 literals; the
other 48, AOT-model fixtures, carry no `class`), through `transformFile`
and then `parseClassLiteral` with `fromClassAttribute` — none is refused. On the
motion side that follows from the strict host: a literal mount reaches and
that does not compile had already failed every command that boots.

Red cases, each watched and reverted. Here, on the code before this patch:
`tests/tailwind.test.ts`'s two new cases and `tests/class-diagnostics.test.ts`'s
rewritten default-mode case; the `&&` case pins behaviour this patch keeps
and is green on both. In the motion repository, `tests/catalog.test.ts`:
with this patch left out of `patches/`, G6 fails 22 probes — the 11
refusals of row `class.unparsed-token` and 11 of the overflow, shutter and
paint rows that asserted `dropsLiteral` under `fromClassAttribute` and now
assert the refusal. With the two `if (!attribute) return null;` replaced by
`return null;`, the same 22 and G6b. With `tokenFault` naming a rewrite as
compiling without asking `unknownUtilities`, G6's `opacity-[1e-7]` probe.
With `compileClasses` rethrowing the first refusal, G6's two-literal probe.
With the `tools/build.ts` hunks reverse-applied, G6b: the build still exits
1, on an uncaught `UncompiledClassError` whose stack names no line of
`main.tsx`.

